{
  "schema": "rain-fly-record/v2",
  "run_id": "FR-b9d17b5abb5c7657df2e708f",
  "plan": {
    "schema": "rain-fly-plan/v2",
    "design": {
      "schema": "rain-fly-design/v3",
      "question": "[scripted] ECHO-05: does weighing a conclusion again on fresh evidence before committing to it make the fly's stated confidence in its explanations more accurate?",
      "hypothesis": "[scripted] A fly that commits to a conclusion at once has a higher Brier score on hidden held-out changes than one that takes a second look first.",
      "competing_hypothesis": "[scripted] The evidence that made the first answer likely also makes the second look agree, so it only delays the same answers.",
      "parameters": {
        "seed_count": 16,
        "treatment": {
          "kind": "echo_metacognition",
          "variant": "no_metacognition"
        }
      },
      "analysis": {
        "method": "matched_seed_sign_test",
        "metric": "attribution_brier",
        "expected_direction": "increase",
        "minimum_effect": 0.2
      },
      "budget": {
        "max_simulated_seconds": 16000
      },
      "provenance": {
        "generation": "scripted",
        "decision_sha256": null,
        "source_ids": [],
        "parent_record_sha256": null
      }
    },
    "ir": {
      "schema": "rain-scientific-experiment/v1",
      "domain": "fly-behavior-simulation",
      "backend": "digital-fly",
      "backend_version": "digital-fly-behavior/v3 (digital-fly/9 reduced-order-fsm/v1)",
      "question": "In the digital fly simulator, how does the ECHO controller with the second look disabled (it commits to a conclusion at once) change mean Brier score of the fly's confidence in each explanation it concluded (0 is perfect), against the full ECHO self-model, which takes a second look before it commits, under the same hidden conditions on matched seeds?",
      "hypothesis": "The ECHO controller with the second look disabled (it commits to a conclusion at once) will increase attribution_brier by at least 0.2 score on average over 16 matched seeds drawn after preregistration, compared with the full ECHO self-model, which takes a second look before it commits, under the same hidden conditions, and an exact one-sided sign test on the seeds that differ will find the increase at alpha 0.05.",
      "observables": [
        {
          "id": "attribution_accuracy",
          "unit": "fraction",
          "description": "fraction of hidden changes (onsets, and the ends of changes to the body or the surroundings) the fly explained with the right cause; a temporary obstacle is answered right by any explanation but its own body; a misleading sense is left out"
        },
        {
          "id": "false_self_attribution",
          "unit": "fraction",
          "description": "fraction of external changes (a push, a turning surface) the fly blamed on its own body"
        },
        {
          "id": "attribution_brier",
          "unit": "score",
          "description": "mean Brier score of the fly's confidence in each explanation it concluded (0 is perfect)"
        },
        {
          "id": "attribution_path_error",
          "unit": "fraction",
          "description": "mean path error of steady walking over the whole run"
        },
        {
          "id": "missed_changes",
          "unit": "count",
          "description": "hidden changes the fly never asked about within 6 s"
        },
        {
          "id": "misleading_sense_blamed_self",
          "unit": "fraction",
          "description": "fraction of misleading-sense onsets (a motion read that is not there, which no sense here can tell from a push) the fly blamed on its body"
        },
        {
          "id": "attribution_coverage_90",
          "unit": "fraction",
          "description": "share of observed self-motion components inside the fly's predicted central 90% interval"
        },
        {
          "id": "mean_paired_delta",
          "unit": "score",
          "description": "treatment minus control attribution_brier, averaged over the seeds where both are defined"
        },
        {
          "id": "sign_test_p_expected",
          "unit": "probability",
          "description": "exact one-sided sign test p-value for a increase, over untied seeds"
        },
        {
          "id": "sign_test_p_opposite",
          "unit": "probability",
          "description": "the same test in the opposite direction"
        }
      ],
      "independent_variables": [
        "the ECHO controller with the second look disabled (it commits to a conclusion at once)"
      ],
      "dependent_variables": [
        "attribution_brier"
      ],
      "controls": [
        "baseline arm: the full ECHO self-model, which takes a second look before it commits, under the same hidden conditions",
        "matched seeds: each seed places the fly, draws its stimuli and seeds its random streams identically in both arms",
        "the seeds are drawn by the host after preregistration from SHA-256 of the registry receipt and a random nonce in the authorization, so neither the design nor the operator chooses them",
        "fixed apparatus settings, both arms — attribution block: groomHz = 0, takeoffHz = 0, walkStartHz = 1, walkStopHz = 0.1",
        "the fly's version, controller, full parameter table and source digest are bound into the plan digest"
      ],
      "parameter_constraints": [
        "11 closed protocols with host-built arenas, stimuli and hidden perturbation schedules",
        "controller configurations override model parameters only, within their ranges; FlyBody and published values are fixed",
        "the integration settings (physics, sensing and decision periods) are never varied",
        "at most 16000 simulated fly-seconds per experiment",
        "8-16 seeds, drawn by the host after preregistration",
        "a minimum effect at or above the host's 0.2 score for attribution_brier"
      ],
      "resource_budget": {
        "execution_steps": 1024000,
        "replay_steps": 1024000,
        "max_samples": 32
      },
      "statistical_analysis": {
        "method": "Exact one-sided sign test on the untied matched seeds at alpha 0.05, with at least 6 untied seeds, and a mean paired difference beyond the minimum effect; frozen rain-criteria/v1",
        "independent_replicates": 16,
        "uncertainty": "The p-values are exact binomial tail probabilities over seeds of one deterministic model: they say how consistently the two arms differ across this simulator's seeds, not how uncertain anything is about a population, and nothing about real flies."
      },
      "falsification": {
        "metric": "sign_test_p_opposite",
        "op": "<=",
        "value": 0.05,
        "unit": "probability"
      },
      "provenance": {
        "generation": "scripted",
        "decision_sha256": null,
        "source_ids": [],
        "parent_record_sha256": null
      },
      "limitations": [
        "The digital fly's controller is a reduced-order state machine. It simulates no neurons; its links to Virtual Fly Brain terms are labels, not mechanisms.",
        "Model parameters were chosen for this simulator, not measured or fitted; a result describes the simulator with these values.",
        "Flight is kinematic: no aerodynamics and no MuJoCo physics run in these experiments.",
        "The arena is a box with primitive obstacles and a relative light proxy (not lux); odour, temperature, humidity, airflow and food are not simulated.",
        "The second look was added after ECHO-02's published held-out results showed the fly overconfident on combined causes, so these conditions are not naive for it; its one parameter was set by a principle, never tuned on them (docs/PROJECT_ECHO.md). Its stated confidence is the Bayesian posterior over the fly's own explanations, a functional analogue of metacognitive monitoring (HOT-2), not a report of felt certainty. Its pilot found no seed count that passes with 0.9 probability, so the demonstration runs under-powered at the host's largest seed count and minimum effect.",
        "The ECHO self-model is a computational model of the fly's own sensorimotor gains, built over the reduced-order body. Its compensation acts on abstract movement gains of that body; it is not validated biological motor control, and FlyBody's physics does not run.",
        "The perturbations are the experimenter's models of an impairment, a push, a turning surface, a sensor fault and an obstacle, applied by the simulator. The fly is never given their labels, causes or schedule; it meets only their consequences through its self-motion sense, its touch and its legs' stepping.",
        "The conditions are the held-out evaluation set, in an arena the self-model was not developed in; its constants were chosen on the development set alone and frozen before evaluation.",
        "A measurement of prediction, attribution or retention describes a functional self-model in a simulator. It is not evidence of awareness or subjective experience.",
        "Re-simulation is exact on the JavaScript engine that made a record; Math functions differ in their last bits between engines, so on another engine a record is reported as not re-simulable rather than checked.",
        "A result characterizes the simulator and is never empirical evidence about Drosophila."
      ]
    },
